        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe new `getCheckDigit()` fits the codebase's established shape cleanly:\n\n- **Right unit.** The `Owner` entity already hosts a family of self-contained derived read-only getters exposed to the API \u2014 `getMembershipLevel()`, `getLocality()`, `getContactPreference()`, plus the `regionOf`/`postcodeRangeOf` helpers. A Luhn digest over this entity's own `customerCode` field belongs in exactly this cluster, and it's placed right among those siblings. There's no separate \"customer code\" service or util that would be a more natural home.\n- **No duplication.** Nothing else in the tree computes Luhn or a check digit (`AssignCustomerCode` only builds the code string; this is the sole derivation consuming it). The algorithm isn't repeated anywhere.\n- **Self-contained and idiomatic.** It reads a single field, handles the `null` case the same way the other derived getters guard their inputs, and the Javadoc matches the house style (precise, cross-linking `{@link #getCustomerCode()}`, stating the no-code fallback). The loop is plain, local, and doesn't leak state.\n- **OpenAPI addition is consistent.** The `checkDigit` property mirrors its sibling derived fields \u2014 `readOnly: true`, title/description/example, and a sensible `minimum: 0`/`maximum: 9` bound for a single digit.\n\n`Owner` is accumulating several derived getters, but that is the pre-existing, deliberate pattern here rather than this change turning it into a catch-all, so the addition reads as natural to a maintainer."
